# AWS changes stay in the repo

- Change infrastructure by editing Terraform, plus the README and changelog entries that go with it. Do not mutate live AWS from a Cursor session: no `authorize-security-group-ingress`, no other console or CLI resource edits, and no `terraform apply` unless the user explicitly asks to apply.
- The metrics interface VPC endpoints (`monitoring`, `secretsmanager`) use private DNS. The server's `PutMetricData` goes to that endpoint. `terraform/modules/metrics` must allow TCP 443 from every security group attached to the EC2 instance, in addition to the metrics Lambda. Lambda-only ingress makes the publish time out (`context deadline exceeded`, HTTP status 0).
- Dev's instance uses the pre-existing groups resolved from `ec2_security_group_names` (`launch-wizard-2`, `ec2-rds-1`). Pass `local.ec2_security_group_ids`. `module.ec2.security_group_id` is null in that case. Prod uses the security group the EC2 module creates, unless `ec2_security_group_ids` is set.
- Dig and prod share one VPC. Only dig creates the `monitoring` and `secretsmanager` interface endpoints (`metrics_create_vpc_endpoints` defaults to false in prod). Prod adds TCP 443 on the existing `dev-ec2-metrics-endpoints` group for its Lambda and EC2 security groups. Do not plan a second private-DNS endpoint for the same service.
- The endpoint security group uses standalone `aws_security_group_rule` resources. An inline `ingress` block would delete rules added from the other environment on the next apply.
- Prod RDS keeps `ingress_allow_self` so the CloudShell self-rule on `e2eetext-prod-rds` is not dropped. The app and CloudShell permissions are standalone `aws_security_group_rule` resources, and the security group ignores `ingress`. An inline ingress block revokes the metrics Lambda rule (`rds_from_lambda`) on the next apply.
